19:42.440 --> 19:50.760
And this overarching perspective aligns beautifully with the philosopher Lawrence Cahoon's work on what he calls local metaphysics or objective relativism.

19:50.860 --> 19:51.800
Okay, tell us about that.

19:51.800 --> 20:01.460
Cahoon argues, essentially, that there's no single, one, true, perfectly homogenous universe that exists independently of any perspective or interaction.

20:02.100 --> 20:06.240
Instead, reality is better understood as a collection of local, effective descriptions.

20:06.560 --> 20:07.540
So context matters.

20:07.720 --> 20:08.480
Relations matter.

20:08.620 --> 20:09.160
Immensely.

20:09.160 --> 20:12.300
Every complex entity is defined by its relations.

20:12.760 --> 20:14.480
Nothing truly exists in isolation.

20:15.220 --> 20:24.600
It's objective because these relations and structures are real, but relative because any description is always from within a particular frame, a particular set of interactions.

20:25.180 --> 20:31.420
So it's not that there's no truth, but that truth is always situated, always relative to the system of relations you're looking at.

20:31.460 --> 20:32.340
That captures it well.

20:32.340 --> 20:38.680
And Cahoon also introduces this idea of distinct orders of nature, physical, material, biological, mental, cultural.

20:38.900 --> 20:45.140
Each one is seen as irreducible, emerging at different thresholds of complexity and, crucially, entropy regulation.

20:45.400 --> 20:47.720
Ah, tying back to pre-grigion and entropy export.

20:48.080 --> 20:48.620
Precisely.

20:49.260 --> 21:02.280
RSVP tries to formalize this idea, seeing these orders as related to entropic phase transitions moments where systems spontaneously organize themselves into new, stable configurations with different ways of managing entropy.

21:02.340 --> 21:06.040
And this changes how we even think about scientific knowledge itself, right?

21:06.200 --> 21:08.220
Like laws versus heuristics.

21:08.520 --> 21:08.860
It does.

21:08.960 --> 21:14.360
In this view, heuristics, rules, laws, they aren't rigid, absolute categories set in stone.

21:14.800 --> 21:19.940
They're more like a spectrum of probabilistic constraints that emerge from the underlying field dynamics.

21:20.140 --> 21:22.260
So laws are just really, really reliable rules.

21:22.660 --> 21:25.220
You could say laws are high-fidelity heuristics.

21:25.700 --> 21:30.140
They represent highly stable, time-average attractors in this underlying field space.

21:30.140 --> 21:39.420
They describe incredibly reliable patterns, but crucially, they're still, in principle, open to revision based on new evidence or a shift in perspective.

21:39.880 --> 21:41.840
It's a deeply fallibilist view of science.

21:42.160 --> 21:43.040
No final word.

21:43.080 --> 21:47.580
And even purposeful behavior, like in biology, fits into this without needing a grand designer.

21:47.900 --> 21:52.700
Yes, through the concept of teleonomy, which is purpose-like organization without foresight.

21:52.700 --> 22:01.220
Think of a simple thermostat maintaining a room's temperature or a living organism maintaining homeostasis, keeping its internal environment stable.

22:01.480 --> 22:02.500
It acts as if it has a purpose.

22:02.680 --> 22:03.060
Exactly.

22:03.520 --> 22:07.120
RSVP models these as homeostatic attractors, essentially.

22:07.740 --> 22:16.060
Entropy minimizing trajectories or pathways that systems naturally fall into and maintain because they are stable configurations in that relational field.

22:16.060 --> 22:20.220
So wrapping this all up, what does this mean for us, for you listening?

22:20.760 --> 22:24.940
The cosmos, just like logic and computation, can be seen as this.

22:25.620 --> 22:33.100
This plenum, maybe full of null signals, perpetually incomplete in any local description, gets somehow coherent within a greater whole.

22:33.100 --> 22:41.760
And this entire framework we've discussed, it seems designed to keep those suppressed paths, those alternative ways of thinking that get shut down by encoding ratchets to keep them alive.

22:41.920 --> 22:45.380
To resist the urge to finalize and lock down models too quickly.

22:45.660 --> 22:53.300
To understand a universe that seems far more dynamic, more relational, more self-organizing than maybe our simpler, smoother models sometimes suggest.

22:53.300 --> 23:01.180
A universe that builds its coherence, not despite incompleteness, but maybe fundamentally because of it.

23:01.440 --> 23:04.040
That incompleteness is the engine of its dynamism.

23:04.240 --> 23:09.540
If we really take this bigger picture to heart, it suggests a profound shift in perspective, doesn't it?

23:09.640 --> 23:09.860
Yeah.

23:09.900 --> 23:13.440
The universe isn't just a collection of objects expanding passively in space.

23:13.660 --> 23:21.920
But it's actively organizing itself through these complex entropic gradients, this cosmic dance of falling outward in the void as an inward pull around matter.

23:21.920 --> 23:32.600
It's a system that continually defines its own boundaries and creates coherence, not by overcoming incompleteness, but by integrating it, by making it part of its very architecture.

23:32.860 --> 23:35.120
Which leads us back to that question for you, our listener.

23:35.480 --> 23:49.260
If all these ordered systems, from circuits to stars to maybe even societies, maintain themselves by offloading their disorder, their entropy, or by internalizing their own incompleteness, what costs are you externalizing in your own life?

23:49.260 --> 23:54.580
What hidden superset is enabling your local order, your stability, your sense of completeness?

23:54.740 --> 23:56.160
It's a deep thought to chew on.

23:56.460 --> 23:57.040
It really is.

23:57.260 --> 24:00.840
It's a deep dive that I think truly changes how you see almost everything.

24:00.960 --> 24:01.820
Thank you for joining us.

24:01.820 --> 24:02.500
Thank you.

24:02.500 --> 24:02.780
Thank you.

24:02.820 --> 24:03.360
Thank you.

24:03.360 --> 24:20.180
Thank you.

